  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The threat: determinism', note: 'If every event follows from prior causes — and brains are physical systems — then your "choice" was fixed before you made it. Physics adds quantum randomness, but randomness is not freedom either.' },
    { math: '2 · Hard determinism: no free will', note: 'Spinoza: men believe themselves free "because they are conscious of their volitions and ignorant of the causes". Honest, austere — and apparently corrosive to responsibility.' },
    { math: '3 · Libertarian free will: agents break the chain', note: 'Genuinely undetermined choices originate in the self. Problems: how does randomness help (a random choice isn\'t "yours" either)? And it strains physics.' },
    { math: '4 · Compatibilism: free will within determinism', note: 'Hume, Frankfurt, Dennett: freedom means acting on YOUR OWN reasons, without coercion — even in a deterministic world. Frankfurt\'s twist: you are free when you WANT your wants (second-order desires). The majority view among philosophers (PhilPapers survey: ~59%).' },
